            {
              "id": "2d59ab50-7b9b-39e7-86b6-187b26c19bc0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or even code execution if an attacker can control the overflow.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strlen((*kv)`, the `strlen` function is called on a dereferenced pointer `kv`. If `kv` points to a non-null-terminated string, this can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. If you are dealing with user input or other untrusted data, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read beyond this limit.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `kv` is a pointer to a string, a safer version of the code might look like this:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len;\nif (kv != NULL) {\n    len = strnlen(*kv, MAX_LEN);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LEN` is a constant that should be set to the maximum expected length of the string. If `kv` is NULL, the code will not attempt to dereference it, avoiding a potential null pointer dereference.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` and `strnlen` functions are part of the standard C library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
