            {
              "id": "2c79b540-cdee-3938-8e4e-6791bfc8d16a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is a common source of buffer overflow vulnerabilities because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that perform bounds checking or otherwise mitigate the associated risks. For C++, the C++ Standard Library provides many safer alternatives to C standard library functions. \n\nIn the case of `sprintf`, a safer alternative is `snprintf`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the size of the destination buffer. This function will not write more characters than this size, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` and add a size parameter. Assuming `(*kv)[*kvnum].name` is a character array of size `NAME_SIZE`, the fixed code would look like this:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name, NAME_SIZE, \"%s\", table[x].field_name);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to only require the C standard library, which is included with any standard C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8ae6e632-eb40-3ad2-a53f-10f2dbb85029",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. The `rewind` function is one such function that can potentially lead to these issues.\n\nThe `rewind` function sets the file position to the beginning of the file for the stream pointed to by stream. It's equivalent to `(void) fseek(stream, 0L, SEEK_SET)`, but `rewind` also clears the error indicator.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `rewind` function if possible. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or that limit the potential for buffer overflows and other issues. For example, you can use the `fseek` function with appropriate error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `rewind`, you can use `fseek` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"file.txt\", \"r\");\nif (fp == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n}\n\n// Instead of rewind(fp);\nif (fseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_SET) != 0) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `rewind` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3dc28579-210a-30f6-9403-757d89613ff6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fprintf`, you can use `fputs` or `fwrite` which do not interpret the format string. Also, always validate and sanitize input data before using it in such functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"[ERROR] Invalid buffer as read length was exceeded\\n\", stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8bd32df0-e207-3950-8873-3e88a34b2490",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fprintf`, consider using `fputs` or `fwrite` which do not interpret the format string. Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"Metrics version not supported\\n\", stderr);\n```\n\nIf you need to include the version number, consider converting it to a string first and then appending it:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string message = \"Metrics version \" + std::to_string(pmmetrics_version) + \" not supported\\n\";\nfputs(message.c_str(), stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `fprintf` and `stderr`\n- `string` for `std::string` and `std::to_string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5ae42019-828f-382e-8ef2-c543c110ffc1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can lead to undefined behavior when using functions like memcpy. Additionally, there are no explicit guards or checks to prevent this overlap, and the source may also be null, increasing the risk. The source is not a string literal or constant, so the overlap risk is not mitigated by the nature of the source. Therefore, this issue should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or unpredictable program behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping memory regions, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination do not overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add assertions or checks as appropriate to enforce it at runtime.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ae539566-f59c-360d-be80-010269be1c3d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In this case, the function `fprintf` is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if user-controlled input is passed as the format string argument.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions that do not perform bounds checking or that can lead to format string vulnerabilities. Instead, use safer alternatives that are less prone to misuse, such as `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`, or `fgets` instead of `gets`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, the `fprintf` function is used to print an error message to `stderr`. Since the format string is a constant string and does not contain any format specifiers, there is no direct vulnerability here. However, to follow best practices and avoid potential issues in the future, you could replace `fprintf` with `fputs`:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"[ERROR]: pm_metrics file not found \\n\", stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "462e6032-38c7-3bae-bf16-6d9570a6cce7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be insecure because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these insecure C functions. For `sprintf`, a safer alternative would be `snprintf`, which takes an additional size argument to ensure that it does not write past the end of the buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name, sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \"%s[%d]\", table[x].field_name, y);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<stdio.h>` for `snprintf`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24b32dbb-ee86-35a5-8821-37a76386e4ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name (`fname`) is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using a function that allows for more secure handling of files. Always validate and sanitize input from untrusted sources. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream infile;\ninfile.open(fname, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\nIn this example, the C++ `ifstream` class is used to open the file in binary mode. This class provides more secure file handling than the C `fopen` function.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "20efa55b-9eab-3e2a-aa57-61fd75040f9a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is a common source of buffer overflow vulnerabilities because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that perform bounds checking or otherwise mitigate the associated risks. For C++, the C++ Standard Library provides many safer alternatives to C standard library functions. \n\nIn the case of `sprintf`, a safer alternative is `snprintf`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the size of the destination buffer. This function will not write more characters than this size, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` and add a size parameter. Assuming `(*kv)[*kvnum].name` is a character array of size `NAME_SIZE`, the fixed code would look like this:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name, NAME_SIZE, \"%s\", table[x].field_name);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to only require the C standard library, which is included with any standard C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "52d12378-9fa2-3879-8298-57b7f97332b6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `fprintf`, you can use `fputs` or `fwrite` which do not interpret the format string. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nfputs(\"[ERROR]: Invalid buffer as buffer length exceeded\\n\", stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
