            {
              "id": "24a671e8-f5c9-370b-8f1c-29f3e9ab32ff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`, or `snprintf` instead of `sprintf`. In the case of `sscanf`, it is recommended to use a combination of other functions such as `strtok` and `strtol` to parse the input string in a safer way.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative to the `sscanf` function:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar *token;\nchar *next_token;\nchar message[] = \"your message here\";\nreset_seq_num = strtol(strtok_s(message, \" \", &next_token), NULL, 16);\nreset_cause = strtok_s(NULL, \"\\n\", &next_token);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `string.h`\n- `stdlib.h`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "63ec1709-e35d-32cf-86df-4063e233c435",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly validated.\n\nThe `sscanf` function is used to read formatted input from a string. The function reads characters from the string, interprets them according to a specified format, and stores the results in its arguments. If the input is not properly validated, an attacker can provide a specially crafted string that can lead to buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `sscanf` function or any other insecure C library functions. Instead, use secure alternatives that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflow. Also, always validate user input to ensure it is within the expected range and format.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sscanf` function with a secure alternative. For example, you can use `strtok` and `strtol` functions to parse the string:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nchar *token = strtok(message, \":\");\nif (token != NULL) {\n    pid = strtol(token, NULL, 16);\n    token = strtok(NULL, \"\\n\");\n    if (token != NULL) {\n        strncpy(task_name, token, sizeof(task_name) - 1);\n        task_name[sizeof(task_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for `strtok` and `strncpy` functions.\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2eb15170-1b3f-3b8f-8e8a-287b235328b4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used carefully. This is because `sscanf` does not perform bounds checking on the input, and can therefore write beyond the end of the buffer if the input is too large.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `sscanf` and similar functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking, such as `strncpy` or `snprintf`. Additionally, always validate and sanitize input before processing it to ensure it does not exceed the size of the buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sscanf` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use `strncpy` and `strtol` to achieve the same result:\n\n```cpp\nchar *endptr;\nevent = strtol(event_in, &endptr, 16);\nstrncpy(message, endptr, sizeof(message) - 1);\nmessage[sizeof(message) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code first converts the hexadecimal number at the start of `event_in` to an integer using `strtol`, then copies the rest of the string into `message` using `strncpy`, ensuring not to exceed the size of `message`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `#include <stdio.h>`\n- `#include <stdlib.h>`\n- `#include <string.h>`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "71c77034-f2d6-3e8d-b7cf-c5fb9c8cc850",
