              "id": "e6c1ef5d-5a8d-36c8-ae99-94952fcaca2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "The code copies a string into a character buffer using a function that requires careful management of buffer sizes. The destination buffer's size is not known at this point, and the length used for copying is not proven to be within the bounds of the destination. This can lead to buffer overflows if the length exceeds the actual buffer size. There are no explicit checks or guards to ensure the destination buffer is large enough, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can also lead to string handling errors. The source is a C-style string, which reduces the risk of overlapping memory regions, but the main concern remains the lack of destination size validation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough for the data being copied. If possible, determine the actual size of the destination buffer and use it as the maximum length for the copy operation. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\nFor example, if you know the size of `name` (e.g., `NAME_SIZE`), use:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), NAME_SIZE - 1);\nname[NAME_SIZE - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf the size is not known, consider refactoring the code to pass the buffer size as an argument, or use safer string handling functions that require the buffer size. Always ensure the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after copying.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4bcffff6-2591-3fec-84ca-559766c225d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(version_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if `version_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter to prevent buffer overflows, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// Ensure version_key is null-terminated\nversion_key[version_key.size() - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Use strnlen instead of strlen\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(version_key, version_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and its member functions.\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen` and `std::strnlen`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0c3f9dc9-a2e1-3d7c-92c3-292b68af3001",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will keep reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(schema_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `schema_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Alternatively, consider using safer string handling functions that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure schema_key is null-terminated\nschema_key[schema_key.size()] = '\\0';\nfeature_line.substr(strlen(schema_key)).c_str();\n```\n\nOr use `strnlen`:\n\n```cpp\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(schema_key, schema_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and `std::string::substr`\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "06734f0b-8cb9-33be-98ef-1dfe2d00eaa2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives. In this case, use `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)` instead of `strerror(errno)`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strerror(errno)` function with `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n    // Use buf\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `string.h`: This library is required for the `strerror_r()` function.\n- `errno.h`: This library is required for the `errno` variable.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "733ef215-28f0-39af-8193-864fcac36116",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives. In this case, use `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)` instead of `strerror(errno)`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strerror(errno)` function with `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n    // Use buf\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `string.h`: This library is required for the `strerror_r()` function.\n- `errno.h`: This library is required for the `errno` variable.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ef5fc429-9096-3e76-8f0a-8b953be2bf42",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable it retrieves is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `getenv` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have these vulnerabilities. If you must use `getenv`, ensure that the returned value is properly sanitized and validated before use. Also, consider using secure coding practices to minimize the risk of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you could use a configuration file to store the required settings, and then read this file at runtime. This would avoid the potential for an attacker to manipulate the environment variable.\n\n```cpp\nstd::ifstream config_file(\"config.txt\");\nstd::string hwmon_root_override;\nif (config_file.is_open())\n{\n    std::getline(config_file, hwmon_root_override);\n    env_vars_.path_HWMon_root_override = hwmon_root_override;\n}\nconfig_file.close();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `getenv`\n- `<fstream>` for `std::ifstream`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6b9b8ae4-e624-325e-94a2-4d216db6314a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the function `getenv()` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv()` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to information disclosure or unauthorized code execution if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, consider using a method that does not involve environment variables to pass sensitive information. If environment variables must be used, ensure they are properly sanitized and validated before use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming the use of Boost library\n#include <boost/optional.hpp>\n\nboost::optional<std::string> get_env(const char* name) {\n    if(const char* env_p = std::getenv(name)) {\n        return env_p;\n    }\n    return boost::none;\n}\n\n// Usage\nauto path_power_root_override = get_env(\"RSMI_DEBUG_PP_ROOT_OVERRIDE\");\nif(path_power_root_override) {\n    env_vars_.path_power_root_override = *path_power_root_override;\n} else {\n    // Handle the case when the environment variable is not set\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- C++ Standard Library\n- Boost Library (optional.hpp)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "877c34d5-3110-3f11-aa93-5409850d5c54",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable it retrieves is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not have the same vulnerabilities. If you must use `getenv`, ensure that the returned value is properly validated and sanitized before use. Also, consider using secure coding practices to minimize the risk of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you could use a configuration file to store the required values, and then read these values using a safe function. Here is an example of how you might do this:\n\n```cpp\nstd::ifstream config_file(\"config.txt\");\nstd::string drm_root_override;\nif (config_file.is_open())\n{\n    std::getline(config_file, drm_root_override);\n    env_vars_.path_DRM_root_override = drm_root_override;\n}\nconfig_file.close();\n```\n\nIn this example, the `RSMI_DEBUG_DRM_ROOT_OVERRIDE` value is read from a configuration file instead of from an environment variable.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstdlib` for the `getenv` function\n- `fstream` for the `ifstream` class\n- `string` for the `string` class\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Code Review Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-code-review-guide/)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c02e4931-3075-348f-8c77-45cab99f07f3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not used carefully. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to serious security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. If you must use them, ensure that you are handling their results and inputs carefully to avoid security issues. In the case of `getenv`, consider the possibility that the environment variable may not exist and that the function will return a null pointer. Also, be aware that the returned string may be modified by subsequent calls to `getenv`, `setenv`, or `unsetenv`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nconst char* ev_str = std::getenv(\"ENV_VAR\");\nif(ev_str != nullptr)\n{\n    // Use ev_str\n}\nelse\n{\n    // Handle the case where ENV_VAR does not exist in the environment\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e35069fe-ec44-3fe7-920d-e67a1f3a96ac",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not used carefully. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to serious security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. If you must use them, ensure that you are handling their results and inputs carefully to avoid security issues. In the case of `getenv`, consider the possibility that the environment variable may not exist and that the function will return a null pointer. Also, be aware that the returned string may be modified by subsequent calls to `getenv`, `setenv`, or `unsetenv`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nconst char* ev_str = std::getenv(\"ENV_VAR\");\nif(ev_str != nullptr)\n{\n    // Use ev_str\n}\nelse\n{\n    // Handle the case where ENV_VAR does not exist in the environment\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
