            {
              "id": "8b028349-07e7-3e69-83ff-2a51fad5fceb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `fscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or limited.\n\nThe `fscanf` function reads data from the `fp` stream and stores them according to the parameter format into the locations pointed by the additional arguments. The additional arguments should point to already allocated objects of the type specified by their corresponding format tag within the format string.\n\nIn the provided code, the `fscanf` function is used to read a double and a string from a file. If the string read from the file is longer than the buffer `buff`, a buffer overflow will occur, which can lead to unpredictable program behavior, including program crashes and security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the `fscanf` function with a safer alternative that includes buffer size checks. For example, you could use `fgets` to read the string and then `sscanf` to parse it. This allows you to control the maximum number of characters read to prevent buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `fgets` and `sscanf`:\n\n```c++\nchar line[256];\nif (fgets(line, sizeof(line), fp) != NULL) {\n    if (sscanf(line, \"%lf %s\", &pcie_speed, buff) != 2) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for file operations and `fscanf`, `fgets`, `sscanf` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "452bbef9-a323-3333-857f-2e7406dd6bdd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, instead of using `fopen`, you can use the `fstream` class from the C++ Standard Library which provides a higher level of abstraction and more control over file operations.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `fstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string path_max_link_speed;\n// ... (initialize path_max_link_speed)\nstd::ifstream file(path_max_link_speed);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for file operations\n- `<string>`: for string operations\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6bd65a1f-58a0-34e3-98de-12fde6daad81",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `fgets` or `fread`, which allow you to specify the maximum number of characters to be read, thus preventing buffer overflow. Also, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent malicious data from causing harm.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fscanf` function with a safer alternative. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[10];\nif (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), fp) != NULL) {\n    pcie_width = atoi(buffer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for file operations\n- `stdlib.h` for the `atoi` function\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A1-Injection](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A1_2017-Injection)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a85c9998-02cd-36ca-aeb9-d947e77f3af9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is manipulated or controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using `fstream` objects from the C++ Standard Library, which provide a higher level of abstraction and are generally safer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` object from the C++ Standard Library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\nstd::ifstream file(path_max_link_width.c_str());\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for the `ifstream` object\n- `<string>`: for the `string` object and its `c_str()` method\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
