            {
              "id": "2fd20e28-e306-3773-9596-5e8a08501aaf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in the call to `strncpy`. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may overlap, which is not allowed by the C standard for `strncpy` and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the call, which can further increase the risk of string-related bugs. The source may also be null at this point, as there is no prior check ensuring it is non-null. These factors together indicate a real risk of undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If there is any possibility of overlap, use a temporary buffer to separate the source and destination. Additionally, always explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Example remediation:\nif (vbios_version != info->version) {\n    strncpy(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n} else {\n    // If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer\n    char temp[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH];\n    strncpy(temp, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    temp[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n    strncpy(info->version, temp, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13437bfe-bd00-3bcd-b8e7-a062cdad5011",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a call to `strncpy`. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard. The source is not a string literal or a constant, and there is no evidence of a guard or explicit null-termination after the call. Additionally, the source may be null at this point, which further increases the risk. The destination is guarded as non-null, but this does not mitigate the overlap risk. The absence of a proven buffer capacity or explicit null-termination after the copy further increases the severity of the issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the `strncpy` call, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, always ensure that the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// If overlap is possible, use memmove and ensure null-termination:\nmemmove(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf you are certain that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and consider adding an assertion to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```cpp\n// Assert non-overlapping buffers (if you are sure):\nassert(info->version != reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str));\nstrncpy(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6420b844-b1a1-3469-bf3d-8f41319de2d7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in the use of `strncpy`. The verdict is supported by the fact that the dataflow analysis indicates a feasible overlap between the destination and source, which can cause undefined behavior according to the C standard. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the call, and the source pointer may be null, further increasing the risk. No evidence of a guard or capacity check is present, and the destination buffer's capacity is not known. These factors all contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the use of `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy operation to prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n```cpp\n// Before:\n// strncpy(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n\n// After:\nmemmove(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n// Ensure null-termination if the destination is used as a string\ninfo->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "575ef44a-f2da-33c7-b357-78915bf2cf8b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in a call to `strncpy`. The verdict is supported by the classification and the explicit 'GENUINE' label. The dataflow analysis suggests that the destination and source may overlap, which can cause undefined behavior with `strncpy`. Although the source is cast from another type and the destination is likely a struct member, the analysis cannot rule out overlap. There is no evidence of a guard to prevent this, and the source may also be null, which further increases the risk. The explicit null-termination after the call is good practice but does not mitigate the overlap risk. Therefore, this is a genuine issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the `strncpy` call, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination overlap, the copy will be performed safely. Always verify that the source pointer is valid and not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
