            {
              "id": "d239e3b3-ecda-3c55-b4b6-57484c08f6f1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" refers to the use of C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\nThe `sscanf()` function is used to read formatted input from a string. The function reads characters from the string, interprets them according to a specified format, and then stores the results in its arguments. If the format string is not properly validated or if the function is used incorrectly, it can lead to buffer overflows or other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sscanf()`. In C++, you can use the `std::istringstream` class from the `<sstream>` library, which provides a safer and more flexible way to parse strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string bdf_str = \"some string\";\nunsigned int domain, bus, device, function;\n\nstd::istringstream iss(bdf_str);\nchar ch; // to read the ':' and '.' characters\n\nif (iss >> std::hex >> domain >> ch >> bus >> ch >> device >> ch >> function) {\n    // parsed successfully\n} else {\n    // parsing failed\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "aff4f22c-8635-3534-9d43-33bdc7de302c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (`strncpy`) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, which prohibits overlapping memory regions for this function. The analysis detected that the source and destination may be related through data flow, increasing the risk of overlap. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, which can result in unterminated strings if the source is longer than or equal to the maximum length. There is also no evidence of a guard to prevent underflow or overlap, and the source pointer may be null at this point, which could cause a crash. These factors all contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling the string copy function. If there is any chance of overlap, use a memory-safe function such as `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping regions. Additionally, always explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure source and destination do not overlap, or use memmove if overlap is possible\nif (info->vram_vendor != brand) {\n    strncpy(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Explicit null-termination\n} else {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove\n    memmove(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nAlso, add a null check for the source pointer before copying:\n\n```cpp\nif (brand != NULL) {\n    // ...copy as above\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bcae3ec3-32ae-3b48-899b-bdaa6677c122",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `strerror(errno)` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in multi-threaded applications. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives. In this case, use `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)` instead of `strerror(errno)`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strerror(errno)` function with `strerror_r(errno, buf, buflen)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n    // Use buf\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `string.h`: This library is required for the `strerror_r()` function.\n- `errno.h`: This library is required for the `errno` variable.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
