            {
              "id": "8b953c96-84da-32f3-a412-7b2b8870a2c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding highlights a real risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the call to `strncpy`. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed by the C standard for `strncpy` and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this overlap, and the source pointer may also be null, which further increases the risk of runtime errors. The destination buffer is a pointer with unknown capacity, and the length argument is not proven to be safe. All these factors indicate that this is a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory with possible overlap, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, always check that the source pointer is not null before copying.\n\n```c\nif (name != proc_id && name != NULL && proc_id != NULL) {\n    strncpy(name, proc_id, len);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    if (len > 0) {\n        name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIf overlap cannot be ruled out, replace `strncpy` with `memmove`:\n\n```c\nif (name != NULL && proc_id != NULL) {\n    memmove(name, proc_id, len);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination if copying strings:\n    if (len > 0) {\n        name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2bf73e0b-ac52-327b-9923-733c6854b5ea",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sprintf` that perform bounds checking, such as `snprintf`. This function takes an additional size argument which specifies the maximum number of bytes to be used in the output buffer, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sprintf` function with `snprintf` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nchar proc_id[PROC_ID_SIZE];\nsnprintf(proc_id, PROC_ID_SIZE, \"%d\", processor->get_processor_index());\n```\n\nIn this code, `PROC_ID_SIZE` is a constant that should be defined to be the size of the `proc_id` buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` or `<stdio.h>` for the `snprintf` function\n- The definition of the `processor` object and its `get_processor_index` method, which are not standard library features and would be defined in your own code or in a third-party library.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links were active and accessible at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f050bab-d8f8-3792-9fa3-e3c6dba22c10",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "The code copies a string from a C++ string's c_str() into a character buffer using strncpy, but the size of the destination buffer is not known at this point, and the length argument is not proven to be within the bounds of the destination. This creates a high risk of buffer overflow if the length exceeds the actual buffer size. There is no evidence of a guard ensuring the length is safe, and the destination is a pointer, making it difficult to verify its capacity. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings if the source is longer than or equal to the specified length. The source is a C string, so overlap and null pointer risks are not present, but the main concern is the lack of bounds checking for the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent buffer overflow, ensure that the length argument used in the copy operation does not exceed the actual size of the destination buffer. If the size of the destination buffer is known (for example, as a constant or macro), use it to limit the number of bytes copied. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\nFor example, if the destination buffer is defined as `char name[64];`, you should use:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t name_capacity = sizeof(name);\nstrncpy(name, socket->get_socket_id().c_str(), name_capacity - 1);\nname[name_capacity - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf the buffer is dynamically allocated, ensure that the length does not exceed the allocated size, and always null-terminate the buffer after copying.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "04820cb6-9f4e-3df5-9ad7-05ae2dd8b59d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backward compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is identified as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation strategy. This function seems to be a custom function, not a standard C or C++ function. If this function is using prohibited C functions internally, it could lead to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the unsafe functions with safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other safety measures. If the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is using unsafe functions internally, you should revise its implementation to use safer alternatives.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the internal implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using unsafe C functions, you should replace them with safer alternatives. For example, if it's using `strcpy`, you could replace it with `strncpy` and make sure to null-terminate the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to determine the specific library dependencies required by the code example. However, if `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is a function from a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
