            {
              "id": "31bd3edb-d331-3f1d-a44d-ef649b2d147e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in a call to `strncpy`. The dataflow analysis suggests that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the C standard for `strncpy` and can result in unpredictable program behavior. The destination buffer is a pointer type, and there is no evidence that the count argument is derived from the destination's capacity, nor is there a guard to prevent underflow or overlap. Although the source and destination are not null at this point, the main concern is the potential for memory overlap, which is a well-known source of subtle bugs and security issues in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when using `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(market_name, name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n```\n\nIf you need to ensure null-termination, explicitly set the last byte to `\\0` after the copy:\n\n```cpp\nmarket_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3baba8f8-759a-3970-a5ba-39e8570654b0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's actual size is not known at this point in the code, and the length argument used in the copy operation is not proven to be safe. The code copies data from a C-style string into a destination buffer using a maximum length that is not directly tied to the actual size of the destination. This can lead to buffer overflows if the destination buffer is smaller than the specified length. There are no explicit checks or guards ensuring that the destination buffer is large enough, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can also lead to string handling issues. The source is a C-style string, which reduces the risk of overlapping memory regions, but the main concern remains the potential for writing beyond the bounds of the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the destination buffer is at least as large as the maximum length specified in the copy operation. If possible, use a fixed-size array for the destination and use `sizeof(destination)` to determine the maximum number of bytes to copy. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\nFor example, if `market_name` is a pointer, ensure it points to a buffer of at least `AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH` bytes before calling the copy operation. After copying, add:\n\n```cpp\nmarket_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf you control the declaration of `market_name`, prefer declaring it as a fixed-size array:\n\n```cpp\nchar market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH];\nstd::strncpy(market_name, empty.c_str(), sizeof(market_name) - 1);\nmarket_name[sizeof(market_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures that the buffer is large enough and always null-terminated, preventing buffer overflows and string handling errors.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "17a0f40a-2e6f-3df8-89a9-ef7793a9a277",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known at this point in the code, and the length argument used for copying (len-1) is not proven to be safe with respect to the actual size of the destination buffer. The use of a pointer type for the destination (char*) without any evidence of its allocated size increases the risk of a buffer overflow. There is no explicit guard to ensure that the length does not exceed the buffer's capacity, and there is no evidence that the length is derived from the buffer's actual size. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The source is a C++ string's c_str(), which is safe from overlap, but this does not mitigate the risk of overflowing the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this issue, ensure that the length argument used in the copy operation does not exceed the actual size of the destination buffer. If possible, determine the size of the destination buffer and use it to limit the number of bytes copied. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated string issues. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Assume 'version' points to a buffer of size 'version_capacity'\nsize_t copy_len = (len - 1 < version_capacity - 1) ? len - 1 : version_capacity - 1;\nstd::strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), copy_len);\nversion[copy_len] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIf the size of the destination buffer cannot be determined, consider redesigning the function to accept the buffer size as an additional parameter, or use safer string handling functions that require the buffer size.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
