            {
              "id": "9267c9ef-d557-3870-b236-c0717cdf9144",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy()` instead of `strcpy()`, `snprintf()` instead of `sprintf()`, and so on. In this case, you can use `std::istringstream` or `std::stoi` instead of `sscanf()`. \n\nAlso, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent buffer overflow and other types of attacks. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::istringstream iss(line);\nstd::string str;\nlong correctable_count;\n\nif (!(iss >> str >> correctable_count)) {\n    // handle error\n}\nerr_cnt->correctable_count = correctable_count;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>` for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "081101ff-8b07-36d5-8183-6accdaa6dda8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the ones provided by the C++ Standard Library. In addition, input validation and sanitization should be performed to ensure that only expected and safe data is processed by the application.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `sscanf` function with a safer alternative. In this case, you can use the `std::istringstream` class from the C++ Standard Library to parse the string:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string str;\nlong uncorrectable_count;\n\nstd::istringstream iss(line);\niss >> str >> uncorrectable_count;\n\nif (iss.fail()) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "434c5099-21c7-3f96-9097-efd3ad40b399",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\nThe `sscanf()` function reads data from `c-string` and stores them according to the parameter format into the locations pointed by the additional arguments. If the user input is not properly validated, it can lead to security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to use these functions in a way that their risks are minimized. For `sscanf()`, it is recommended to use other safer functions such as `strtol()` or `atoi()`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::stoi()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\ntry {\n    int threshold = std::stoi(line);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument const &e) {\n    // The input was not a valid integer\n} catch (std::out_of_range const &e) {\n    // The integer was out of the range of an int\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: This library is required for the `std::string` type and `std::stoi()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "29849bce-ad40-33cc-ae44-821fe07407fd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used carefully. The `sscanf` function does not perform bounds checking, and if the input is not properly validated, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sscanf` that perform bounds checking, or to ensure that the input is properly validated before it is passed to `sscanf`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `sscanf` in C++ could be to use the `std::istringstream` class from the `<sstream>` library. Here is how you could rewrite the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string line;\nunsigned int dpm_level;\nint freq;\nchar str;\n\nstd::istringstream iss(line);\nif (!(iss >> dpm_level >> freq >> str)) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
