            {
              "id": "ec8bcf61-adfe-35b4-afea-dd2e74aced80",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. In this case, the function `sscanf()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the input is not properly controlled or validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `strncpy()` instead of `strcpy()`, `snprintf()` instead of `sprintf()`, and so on. In this case, you can use `std::istringstream` or `std::stoi` to parse the string safely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::istringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::istringstream iss(line);\nif (!(iss >> dpm_level >> freq >> str) || iss.eof()) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::istringstream`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b47e734c-ec04-32e1-97aa-a5921758ce6d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. In this case, the `sscanf` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\nThe `sscanf` function reads data from the `val` string, interprets it according to a specified format, and stores the results in the locations pointed by `cap`. If the input is not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sscanf` whenever possible. If `sscanf` must be used, ensure that the input is properly validated and sanitized to prevent potential vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `sscanf` could be the use of `strtol` function, which also converts a string to an integer, but it provides better error checking:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong int value = strtol(val, &endptr, 10);\n\nif (endptr == val) {\n    // handle error: no digits were found\n} else if (*endptr != '\\0') {\n    // handle error: further characters after number\n} else if ((value == LONG_MIN || value == LONG_MAX) && errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle error: out of range\n} else {\n    *cap = static_cast<int>(value);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` for `sscanf`\n- `<cstdlib>` for `strtol`\n- `<cerrno>` for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2a44b698-f2c8-347c-8d06-3d625583e114",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known at this point in the code, and the count argument used in the copy operation is not proven to be safe. The use of a pointer for the destination buffer without a known size increases the risk of a buffer overflow if the provided size is incorrect. Although the source is a C-style string and there is an explicit null-termination after the copy, there is no evidence that the size argument accurately reflects the actual capacity of the destination buffer. This makes it possible for the copy operation to write beyond the bounds of the buffer, leading to potential security issues such as buffer overflows. No underflow guard or capacity check is present, and the count is not derived from the actual buffer size.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the size argument used in the copy operation does not exceed the actual capacity of the destination buffer. If possible, use `sizeof(buff)` when `buff` is a local array, or explicitly pass the buffer's capacity as an argument. If `buff` is a pointer, ensure it points to a memory region of at least `sizeOfBuff` bytes, and validate `sizeOfBuff` before the copy. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Before copying, validate that sizeOfBuff matches the actual buffer size\nif (buff != nullptr && sizeOfBuff > 0) {\n    std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff - 1);\n    buff[sizeOfBuff - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nIf the buffer's size cannot be determined, consider using safer string handling functions or C++ string objects to avoid manual buffer management.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "af112287-6a24-3697-9cc7-4ccb1dab87a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backward compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is identified as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation strategy. This function seems to be a custom function, not a standard C or C++ function. If this function is using prohibited C functions internally, it could lead to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the unsafe functions with safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other safety measures. If the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is using unsafe functions internally, you should revise its implementation to use safer alternatives.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the internal implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using unsafe C functions, you should replace them with safer alternatives. For example, if it's using `strcpy`, you could replace it with `strncpy` and make sure to null-terminate the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to determine the specific library dependencies required by the code example. However, if `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is a function from a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
              "properties": {
